turning now to a story about a police  officer who uses poetry to explore his  complicated feelings about duty and  identity i spoke with my friend officer  sean bland of the inglewood new jersey  police department officer bland is a  24-year veteran of the force and also a  poet with the book of his own poems  coming out next year we discuss what  it's like being a black police officer  in america and also what he believes  needs to change and how putting his  feelings on paper helps him heal  have you  walked outside your door and thought to  yourself i could die today  yeah  yeah i i have have you been in shootouts  before one  what was that like it's uh very surreal  uh things slow down i remember not being  afraid  because there was no time for emotion  it was just time to react and to live  and to survive  reality set in yeah and i was like  saying oh that was you know we almost  didn't make it englewood police veteran  sean bland still carries with him the  emotions brought on by that encounter 20  years ago in the abyss i embrace death  my disease these days he channels those  emotions through a pen and a pad put  down my shovel stop digging my ditch  desire for dying no longer i itch and  how has poetry served as an outlet for  you as a person and a cop well as a  person it's been an outlet an emotional  release being a cop is not like being an  accountant  your experiences what you experience  from day to day those experiences pile  up like this in real time if those  experiences build up without you being  able to interpret them and without you  being able to resolve them for yourself  you're going to overload how does being  a police officer  affect your mental health to be quite  honest man i got diagnosed with  post-traumatic stress disorder years ago  so i go to counseling man you know i go  to therapy to help me manage  because  i need to be well  bland a husband and father of two wrote  monster with his children in mind i'm  trying to survive in america defenseless  to be black a poem that tackles the  intersection of his identity as an  african-american and a cop in america my  son my daughter inherited my fear  i can't promise their safety but they  listen they hear  and then you went on my children also  american but not yet free  framed at conception america's public  enemy  usually when uh  black father is talking to their kids  about being black in america  they don't speak from a perspective of  being a police officer what was it like  when you're talking to  your kids about  being young and being black  and possibly being looked at as a  monster that's a heavy conversation  that's a conversation that my  counterpart in america does not have  with his children to have a conversation  with your son to say hey listen man you  have to carry yourself a certain way  you're 15 years old you're put together  like a man people are gonna be a little  threatened by that  i remember calling you when i was ready  to have that conversation with my son  that's right nate and nehemiah  and  you gave me a whole checklist  he said if they're driving  turn down the car radio  roll down the windows put the light on  hands at 10 and two absolutely because  policemen we're looking for certain  markers if you do something to arouse  them so that their  antennas go up it's gonna be very hard  for you to do anything next to make  those antennas go down  [Applause]  the murder of george floyd by convicted  ex-police officer derek shovin  recharged the demand for police reform i  got to ask you straight up  do you believe that defunding the police  is a good idea i absolutely do not  i think that uh it needs to be harder to  become a cop  i think that  the standards need to go way  up  i think that the training should be  longer i think that uh  the background checks should be more  thorough but i don't think the funding  the police is the answer man but  to people who are on the outside looking  in  that doesn't satisfy i've given a lot of  thought to this i truly believe that any  time a police officer is involved in a  gun related issue a shooting a death of  any civilian i truly believe that the  federal government should be the  investigators i mean law enforcement  works when you're actually enforcing the  law that's when it works  well said now that phrase defund the  police has become politically charged  and does not mean disband police  departments instead some communities are  pushing to recalib reallocate those  funds and resources to other forms of  public safety and community support  officer bland told us that we need to  bridge that gap between police and the  communities that they serve um you know  and he makes a valid point you know he  spoke about the men and women that serve  these communities and how we need to  create more initiatives so that  relationship between the police in those  communities aren't so strained yeah the  messaging on defund the police is has  not been helpful and i love what i love  what your friend said that law  enforcement works when we actually  enforce the law i also like sean bland  yes i also like that he said that he  gets therapy nade yeah i think that's  such an important message to send that  when you're dealing with something  especially for black men in particular  yeah the fact that he could speak about  it so candidly and that it has helped  him he seems like a good man and we need  more good men and women going into law  enforcement yeah and so that may not be  defund the police it could be quite the  opposite paying a wage and providing the  training to make officers as get the  best people and then make them their  best selves yeah make it harder to  become a he said a lot of good  stuff and he said make it harder yes the  requirements of becoming a police  officer and then maintaining that badge  which also using those funds to help  with the mental health and making sure  they're in the right state of mind while  they go out there to protect and serve  us has he helped you you seem to have a  relationship yeah i call him whenever i  have an issue wherever i have a question  he's the guy that i lean on he's he's  more than a friend he's like a brother  does he help you get out of speeding  tickets no  i just don't speak okay

